+# Local connection lifetime extension for libzmq 4.3.4
+
+This downstream extension binds an accepted TCP/IPC stream to ZAP, received
+messages and disconnect events before the first application request. It also
+provides an opt-in ROUTER send guard against routing-ID reuse. It does not change
+the ZAP multipart body, ZMTP wire protocol or application payloads. An unmodified
+DEALER can connect to the modified server library.
+
+Build libzmq with `-DENABLE_DRAFTS=ON`. Consumers using the macros, version-2
+monitor or ROUTER option below must compile with `ZMQ_BUILD_DRAFT_API` defined
+and use the matching headers and library. These extensions are not upstream
+libzmq 4.3.4 APIs. Checking only `zmq_version()` is insufficient.
+
+## Rationale: authentication succeeds before the first message
+
+Coverage: **AC1.4, AC1.6, AC1.10**. The source extension supplies the common
+connection identity needed for cleanup under the selected authentication-time
+session lifecycle. It is not needed merely to obtain an accepted FD.
+
+Unmodified libzmq 4.3.4 already reports the accepted transport FD through
+ZMQ_EVENT_ACCEPTED. Its ZAP request exposes the peer credentials, but neither
+that FD nor a shared connection-lifetime identifier. A received application
+message can provide User-Id and FD together, using message metadata and the
+existing ZMQ_SRCFD accessor. That last association exists only if a message
+actually reaches the Gateway receiver.
+[Monitor events](https://libzmq.readthedocs.io/en/latest/zmq_socket_monitor.html),
+[4.3.4 message metadata](https://github.com/zeromq/libzmq/blob/v4.3.4/src/stream_engine_base.cpp),
+[4.3.4 ZAP request construction](https://github.com/zeromq/libzmq/blob/v4.3.4/src/zap_client.cpp).
+
+The distinction between connection readiness and first-message delivery is
+essential. The transport handshake and ZAP exchange happen during connection
+setup. client_connect returns CLIENT_OK after handshake success; this does not
+require the application to submit a Crypto RPC or confirm that Gateway has
+received any application message. The application may immediately disconnect
+one of its Clients while keeping its other connections open. A transport failure
+can also occur before its first queued message reaches Gateway.
+
+Gateway already creates an independent ApplicationContext and completes
+NotifyAppState(CONNECTED) during authentication, before ZAP success. That context
+needs matching cleanup even if no RPC follows. This failure does not require a
+shared-memory allocation to exist: the authenticated context and Crypto session
+are enough to make targeted disconnect handling necessary.
+
+### Failure sequence without a shared transport identity
+
+Clients A and B below are two DEALER sockets in the same application process,
+with identical UID/GID/PID. The diagram shows B's authentication being processed
+first to illustrate independent scheduling. The A/B labels describe the actual
+connections for the reader; they are not extra information available to the ZAP
+handler. Neither connection sends an application message in this sequence.
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+    participant A as Client A
+    participant B as Client B - same process
+    participant T as Gateway libzmq
+    participant M as Gateway monitor PAIR
+    participant Z as Gateway ZAP handler
+    participant R as Gateway registry
+    participant C as Private lifecycle worker / ICrypto
+    A->>T: Open UDS connection A
+    T-->>M: ACCEPTED with FD 42
+    M->>R: Record accepted FD 42
+    B->>T: Open UDS connection B
+    T-->>M: ACCEPTED with FD 43
+    M->>R: Record accepted FD 43
+    Note over T,R: Monitor and ZAP processing have no shared per-connection key
+    T->>Z: ZAP for B, shared peer credentials, no FD or lifetime
+    Z->>R: Create context U1 from trusted credentials
+    R->>C: Scoped NotifyAppState(context for U1, CONNECTED)
+    C-->>R: Completed successfully
+    R-->>Z: Context published
+    Z-->>T: ZAP 200 with User-Id U1
+    T-->>B: Handshake succeeds, client_connect returns CLIENT_OK
+    T->>Z: ZAP for A, identical peer credentials, no FD or lifetime
+    Z->>R: Create context U2 from trusted credentials
+    R->>C: Scoped NotifyAppState(context for U2, CONNECTED)
+    C-->>R: Completed successfully
+    R-->>Z: Context published
+    Z-->>T: ZAP 200 with User-Id U2
+    T-->>A: Handshake succeeds, client_connect returns CLIENT_OK
+    Note over A,R: No received RPC or probe has associated either User-Id with an FD
+    B->>T: Close only connection B before its first message arrives
+    T-->>M: DISCONNECTED with FD 43
+    M->>R: Resolve FD 43 to its authenticated context
+    Note over R,C: U1 and U2 share credentials, so the registry cannot safely choose one
+    Note over A,R: Closing both would invalidate live A<br/>Retaining both would leave B's session unreclaimed
+```
+
+Adding UID/GID/PID to the disconnect event identifies the process but still
+does not distinguish U1 from U2. Pairing the first ACCEPTED event with the first
+ZAP request is also insufficient: independent queues and I/O threads do not
+provide that ordering contract. The FD can identify the disconnected transport
+while the application still lacks its association with the authenticated session.
+
+### Why an immediate request or automatic probe does not close the gap
+
+ZMQ_PROBE_ROUTER=1 can make DEALER automatically send an empty message when a
+connection is established. It defaults to 0. This is a one-time probe, not a
+periodic heartbeat; Gateway would have to recognize it separately from an RPC.
+If received, it can help associate routing identity, User-Id and FD. It is not
+enabled by the current client design.
+[ZMQ_PROBE_ROUTER](https://libzmq.readthedocs.io/en/latest/zmq_setsockopt.html#zmq-probe-router-bootstrap-connections-to-router-sockets).
+
+Neither a probe nor an application HELLO is guaranteed to reach Gateway before
+the connection fails. Successful zmq_send only establishes local queue
+acceptance. A successful transport handshake is not an acknowledgement that
+Gateway's application loop has consumed the probe. The failure sequence above
+therefore also applies when a first message was queued but never received.
+[Send completion contract](https://libzmq.readthedocs.io/en/latest/zmq_send.html).
+
+An explicit HELLO/ACK protocol could define a different session-activation
+boundary and defer Crypto CONNECTED until Gateway receives HELLO. That would
+require a new bootstrap contract and rules for all pre-HELLO state. It would
+change the selected requirement to bind the context and notify Crypto during
+ZAP. The source extension satisfies that existing requirement without depending
+on a new application message. FD and outgoing-route reuse also require lifetime
+checks independently of when the first message arrives.
+
+### Selected sequence with the connection-lifetime extension
+
+The extension assigns the lifetime before authentication and carries it through
+local ZAP metadata, received messages and monitor events. The registry can bind
+the FD to User-Id before acknowledging ZAP. The sequence below shows B closing
+without an RPC while A remains live.
+
+```mermaid
+sequenceDiagram
+    participant B as Client B
+    participant T as Gateway libzmq
+    participant M as Gateway monitor PAIR
+    participant Z as Gateway ZAP handler
+    participant R as Gateway registry
+    participant C as Private lifecycle worker / ICrypto
+    Note over R,C: Client A already has independent context U2, FD 42, lifetime 101
+    B->>T: Open UDS connection B
+    T->>T: Capture credentials, assign FD 43 and lifetime 102
+    T-->>M: ACCEPTED with FD 43 and lifetime 102
+    M->>R: Record transport lifetime 102 in the current activation
+    T->>Z: ZAP credentials plus local FD 43 and lifetime 102 metadata
+    Z->>R: Bind User-Id U1 to FD 43 and lifetime 102
+    R->>C: Scoped NotifyAppState(context for U1, CONNECTED)
+    C-->>R: Completed successfully
+    R-->>Z: Context ready, binding established
+    Z-->>T: ZAP 200 with User-Id U1
+    T-->>B: Handshake succeeds, client_connect returns CLIENT_OK
+    Note over B,R: No application RPC or probe is required to establish this binding
+    B->>T: Close connection B before sending any RPC
+    T-->>M: DISCONNECTED with FD 43, peer credentials and lifetime 102
+    M->>R: Resolve current activation and lifetime 102, verify stored FD
+    R->>R: Mark only U1 closing, finish its accepted work if any
+    R->>C: Scoped NotifyAppState(context for U1, DISCONNECTED)
+    C-->>R: Completed successfully
+    R->>R: Retire U1 and its releasable resources
+    Note over R,C: Client A and context U2 remain active
+    opt A later connection reuses the numeric FD
+        T->>T: Reuse FD 43 with a new lifetime 103
+        T-->>M: ACCEPTED with FD 43 and lifetime 103
+        M->>R: Track the new lifetime independently
+        Note over M,R: Old lifetime 102 events or cleanup work cannot select lifetime 103
+    end
+```
+
+The shown ordering is one valid execution, not a requirement that monitor
+events arrive before ZAP. Both paths use the same lifetime, so the registry can
+merge their evidence without guessing. If disconnect overtakes an in-progress
+authentication or CONNECTED job, retain a CLOSING record for that lifetime.
+After the job settles, send matching DISCONNECTED if CONNECTED succeeded;
+late authentication completion must not reactivate the closed context.
+
+The non-reused lifetime is also necessary after the initial binding: a delayed
+event, retained job or cleanup action keyed only by FD could otherwise select
+a replacement connection. Carry the activation/lifetime tuple through every
+such record. For outgoing responses, check the original lifetime against the
+currently selected ROUTER pipe before sending, as specified under Guarding an outgoing ROUTER reply. The patch
+provides the transport evidence and send guard; Gateway must implement the
+registry transitions and resource cleanup that consume them.
+
+## Identity and lifetime
+
+Each stream engine gets a nonzero, monotonically allocated `uint64_t` identity
+before its handshake. Allocation is serialized across I/O threads and contexts
+in the loaded libzmq instance. A counter wrap terminates instead of reusing an
+identity. Reusing an FD or a ROUTER routing ID does not reuse this identity.
+
+The identity is local to the library instance and process. Pair it with the
+Gateway activation generation; it is not persistent across process restart or
+library unloading. The client's transport identity and FD are unrelated to the
+server's. Neither is a credential, an application ID or the ZAP `User-Id`.
+
+For Linux UDS, UID/GID/PID are captured with `SO_PEERCRED` while the FD belongs
+to the engine. Disconnect reporting uses this snapshot, without parsing an IP
+address or looking up credentials on a possibly reused FD. Unavailable
+credential fields are `UINT64_MAX`; TCP does not claim UDS credentials.
+
+## Local message metadata
+
+Every body frame received by the local ZAP handler has the following metadata,
+also available on received application messages and ROUTER routing frames:
+
+| Macro / property string | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| `ZMQ_MSG_PROPERTY_CONNECTION_ID` / `Transport-Connection-Id` | Decimal connection lifetime, nonzero uint64 |
+| `ZMQ_MSG_PROPERTY_TRANSPORT_FD` / `Transport-FD` | Decimal transport FD, observed only |
+| `Peer-Address` | Existing peer address, including UDS credentials on Linux |
+
+Read the properties with `zmq_msg_gets()` and copy/parse them before closing the
+message. `zmq_msg_get(..., ZMQ_SRCFD)` continues to work, including on ZAP frames,
+but new code should use the named FD property. The ZAP request-ID frame remains
+an opaque authentication-exchange value; it is not the transport lifetime.
+
+```c
+const char *connection_id = zmq_msg_gets(
+    &zap_frame, ZMQ_MSG_PROPERTY_CONNECTION_ID);
+const char *transport_fd = zmq_msg_gets(
+    &zap_frame, ZMQ_MSG_PROPERTY_TRANSPORT_FD);
+/* Validate and copy both before zmq_msg_close(&zap_frame). */
+```
+
+The ZAP handler assigns its own unique `User-Id` and records its binding to this
+FD/lifetime before returning success. `User-Id` becomes available on application
+messages after authentication. Native transport properties take precedence over
+properties supplied by the ZAP reply or the remote peer.
+
+These are local `zmq_msg_t` properties, not extra ZAP frames or metadata sent
+over the network. A ZAP proxy that rebuilds messages from byte strings loses
+them unless it explicitly preserves them within the trusted local path. The
+direct inproc ZAP REP handler is the intended deployment.
+
+## Version-2 monitor values
+
+The normal v2 framing is preserved:
+`[event:uint64][value_count:uint64][values...][local endpoint][remote endpoint]`.
+Integer frames use the monitor API's native byte order.
+
+| Event for the TCP/IPC stream path | Values, in order |
+| --- | --- |
+| `ZMQ_EVENT_ACCEPTED` | FD, connection ID (`value_count = 2`) |
+| `ZMQ_EVENT_DISCONNECTED` | FD, UID, GID, PID, connection ID (`value_count = 5`) |
+
+Version-1 framing and its single FD value are unchanged. Other event types keep
+their existing values. Event producers without a stream identity retain their
+original single-value format; consumers must reject unsupported bindings rather
+than guess. This extension does not promise the complete binding for WebSocket,
+UDP, inproc or arbitrary transport implementations.
+
+The first four DISCONNECTED values retain the credential extension's ordering,
+but ACCEPTED now has a second value. Update any v2 consumer that assumes every
+event has exactly one value. This patch replaces the previous credential-only
+patch; do not apply both patches to the same source tree.
+
+Use `(activation generation, connection ID)` to resolve a context and verify
+its stored FD. Do not select every connection matching a PID. ZAP, monitor and
+request queues have independent scheduling: retain a closing record when a
+disconnect overtakes authentication; late ZAP success must not reopen it.
+Authentication rejection and closure before the first RPC still carry the
+original identity. A peer closing before ZAP has monitor identity only.
+
+Explicit server shutdown must retire its own contexts. This patch does not add
+DISCONNECTED events for every locally terminated engine, reliable replay of
+monitor events or a remote cleanup acknowledgement.
+
+## Guarding an outgoing ROUTER reply
+
+`ZMQ_ROUTER_EXPECTED_CONNECTION_ID` is a downstream draft socket option with
+local numeric value `10001`; it is not an upstream-assigned option number.
+Its argument is a native `uint64_t`. Zero disables the guard, which is the
+default. A nonzero value persists until explicitly replaced or reset.
+
+Set it on the ROUTER owner thread immediately before each reply's routing frame:
+
+```c
+uint64_t expected = original_request_connection_id;
+int rc = zmq_setsockopt(router, ZMQ_ROUTER_EXPECTED_CONNECTION_ID,
+                       &expected, sizeof(expected));
+if (rc == 0) {
+    rc = zmq_send(router, route, route_size, ZMQ_SNDMORE | ZMQ_DONTWAIT);
+    if (rc >= 0) {
+        rc = zmq_send(router, reply, reply_size, ZMQ_DONTWAIT);
+    }
+}
+/* Check every result. Do not send the body after the routing frame fails. */
+```
+
+The ROUTER records the native transport identity from the stream's handshake
+identity message. At routing-frame admission, it checks the currently selected
+pipe on the same socket-owner thread. A missing, unsupported or different
+identity fails with `EHOSTUNREACH`, even if ROUTER_MANDATORY is disabled. Failure
+does not enter multipart-send state, so the next complete reply can be attempted
+normally. The body then uses that selected pipe, never a new lookup by route.
+
+Wrong option size returns `EINVAL`; changing it after the routing frame but
+before the final body returns `EFSM`. Normal HWM and routing errors still apply.
+Use `ROUTER_MANDATORY=1`, `ROUTER_HANDOVER=0` and nonblocking sends in Gateway.
+A successful send still means local queue acceptance, not peer receipt.
+
+The guard is intended for a binding ROUTER and accepted ZMTP 3.x TCP/IPC peers.
+A ROUTER route without handshake transport metadata fails closed when guarded.
+Existing unguarded ROUTER behavior is unchanged. Gateway must check that setting
+this option succeeds; an unpatched library must not silently disable protection.
+
+## Regression test
+
+The Linux CMake build registers `test_connection_lifetime` when draft APIs,
+IPC and SO_PEERCRED are enabled. The test requires C++11; the extension's library
+code does not introduce a C++11 atomic or a 64-bit libatomic dependency.
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build -DENABLE_DRAFTS=ON -DBUILD_TESTS=ON
+cmake --build build --target test_connection_lifetime
+ctest --test-dir build -R '^test_connection_lifetime$' --output-on-failure
+```
+
+It covers multiple connections from one process with three libzmq I/O threads,
+ZAP/request/event correlation, disconnect without an RPC, rejected and unfinished
+authentication, close before ZAP, actual FD reuse, distinct IDs after context
+recreation, v1 compatibility, native metadata precedence and stale reply rejection
+after an intentionally forced ROUTER handover. Target-device validation remains
+necessary for the deployed library and Gateway integration.
+
+## Applying in the RDK build
+
+The source baseline is the upstream `zeromq-4.3.4.tar.gz` release selected by
+the RDK `rdk-4.0.0` meta-openembedded recipe. Its SHA-256 is
+`c593001a89f5a85dd2ddf564805deb860e02471171b3f204944857336295c3e5`.
+Keep the recipe's existing libsodium-path CMake patch. Replace any previous
+credential-only disconnect patch with `zmq_connection_lifetime.patch`.
+
+For a product-layer `zeromq_4.3.4.bbappend`, place the patch in the adjacent
+`files/` directory and add:
+
+```bitbake
+FILESEXTRAPATHS:prepend := "${THISDIR}/files:"
+SRC_URI:append = " file://zmq_connection_lifetime.patch"
+EXTRA_OECMAKE:append = " -DENABLE_DRAFTS=ON"
+```
+
+Rebuild libzmq and its development package/SDK; rebuilding Gateway alone cannot
+add this functionality. Keep the platform's selected libsodium configuration.
+This snippet does not change package installation or product rootfs policy.
+
+For an extracted source tree, apply with:
+
+```sh
+patch -p1 < /path/to/zmq_connection_lifetime.patch
+```
+
+The patch changes libzmq and supplies a CMake regression test and this document.
+Gateway integration must read/copy the properties, maintain lifetime-to-User-Id
+bindings, decode the extended monitor values and set/check the reply guard.
+The patch does not implement Gateway connection-context cleanup by itself.
+
+## Regression requirements
+
+Validate the patched library against the matching upstream source and selected
+build configuration. Keep observed test results in separate validation reports,
+not in this implementation specification.
+
+Required coverage includes:
+
+- Draft-enabled metadata, guarded replies and both monitor formats, including
+  independent connections from one process, closure before the first request,
+  actual FD reuse and recreated contexts.
+- Existing PLAIN, NULL, ZAP, metadata, monitor, ROUTER and IPC behavior, plus the
+  selected cryptographic dependency configuration.
+- Draft-disabled builds retaining the unchanged stable API and monitor behavior.
+- Product build integration and the installed runtime: option-10001 probing,
+  the private-IPC startup self-test, fail-closed admission and Gateway consumers
+  of authentication/message/event metadata.
